WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.500
Aw, man, there's a new Thor movie? I wonder what it'll be about?

00:00:03.500 --> 00:00:06.390
I mean, they've already had him fighting Loki and the Dark Elves

00:00:06.390 --> 00:00:12.000
What could possibly be next? Oh, I know maybe they'll mention that time that Thor nuked China

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:16.620
Maybe they'll tell the story of that time that Thor helped Hitler kill Stalin, Yeah!

00:00:16.860 --> 00:00:20.140
Those are the MCU movies that I'd be really interested to see.

00:00:37.360 --> 00:00:40.400
Hello Internet, Welcome to Film Theory

00:00:40.500 --> 00:00:44.000
The show that keeps digging until it finds the stuff that everyone else is hoping you forget.

00:00:44.200 --> 00:00:47.500
So after ruining Ant-Man and Spider Man , I figured it was time for a change.

00:00:47.600 --> 00:00:52.000
Time to tackle a Superhero who isn't themed after something you crush under your boot.

00:00:52.100 --> 00:00:55.390
That's right. Today, we're talking Thor Ragnarok

00:00:55.400 --> 00:00:58.620
And I'm happy to report that my shameless groveling at the feet of Disney

00:00:58.620 --> 00:01:01.000
Did finally get me into an early preview of one of their movies.

00:01:01.500 --> 00:01:04.000
Have no fear, today's theory is a hundred percent spoiler free.

00:01:04.500 --> 00:01:09.408
Now in the latest trailer for Thor Ragnarok, they tease a moment that served as a running joke for the movie.

00:01:09.408 --> 00:01:11.180
"Voice activation required."
"Thor"

00:01:11.890 --> 00:01:13.140
"Strongest Avenger"

00:01:13.140 --> 00:01:14.960
"Let me try. Banner"

00:01:14.960 --> 00:01:16.380
"Welcome, strongest Avenger"

00:01:16.380 --> 00:01:17.100
"Uh, what?"

00:01:17.100 --> 00:01:22.900
Thor consistently claims that is the strongest Avenger, but gets undermined time and time again by the Hulk.

00:01:22.900 --> 00:01:25.300
It's hilarious, and it also got me thinking.

00:01:25.300 --> 00:01:28.420
Of the two, Who would be the stronger Avenger?

00:01:28.420 --> 00:01:34.100
Obviously the Quinn jet computer has been programmed to think that Hulk is the strongest, But Thor is literally a god.

00:01:34.300 --> 00:01:37.860
So shouldn't he win the title? Stan Lee certainly seems to think so.

00:01:37.860 --> 00:01:41.760
In a radio interview asking about who would win in a fight, Stan went on record to say,

00:01:41.760 --> 00:01:49.420
"I would have to say Thor because, as strong as Hulk is he's still a mortal, but Thor is one of the Norse gods"

00:01:49.760 --> 00:01:53.000
So who's right here. Does Stan Lee truly know his own creation,

00:01:53.000 --> 00:01:56.280
or will we prove today that he's more out of touch than a parent trying to

00:01:56.300 --> 00:02:00.480
understand what a Ricegum is. Is Thor right to feel slighted by the computer,

00:02:00.480 --> 00:02:04.380
Or is it just stating a fact that this arrogant Asgardian refuses to accept?

00:02:04.380 --> 00:02:09.680
It's time to settle this superhuman grudge match once and for all with the greatest superpower of them all:

00:02:10.300 --> 00:02:10.800
Research.

00:02:10.800 --> 00:02:11.940
First, let me make it clear:

00:02:11.950 --> 00:02:18.069
I want to determine who is literally the strongest, not who would win in a fight since that involves strategy and fighting techniques.

00:02:18.069 --> 00:02:25.179
I'm talking pound for pound who is physically stronger. Who's able to lift more and punch harder, so let's start at the very beginning,

00:02:25.179 --> 00:02:27.179
which I hear is a very good place to start:

00:02:27.540 --> 00:02:32.840
The comics. Across their mutual fifty-five year history, these two Goliath's have gone toe-to-toe a lot.,

00:02:32.860 --> 00:02:38.320
so maybe by seeing who wins in these fights more often we can start to figure out who is the stronger Superhero

00:02:38.340 --> 00:02:39.600
Their first fight,

00:02:39.600 --> 00:02:44.260
1963's Avengers No. 3. No clear winner. Okay, their second fight,

00:02:44.750 --> 00:02:50.199
1965's Journey into Mystery No. 112. No clear winner. Okay, third time's a charm,

00:02:50.860 --> 00:02:58.120
1971's The Submariner 35. No clear winner. Defenders 10. Draw. Incredible Hulk 300.

00:02:58.120 --> 00:03:02.800
Draw. Mighty Thor 489. Draw. By this point you might be noticing a pattern

00:03:02.860 --> 00:03:08.060
The comics want to keep the winner of this battle more vague than a Scott Cawthon storyline.

00:03:08.100 --> 00:03:11.880
In nearly every matchup the two either stalemate each other,

00:03:11.880 --> 00:03:15.780
or the US government comes in and drops a nuke to stop the two from fighting.

00:03:16.030 --> 00:03:18.760
That's your taxpayer dollars at work Ladies and Gentlemen.

00:03:18.760 --> 00:03:23.080
However, There are a few pieces of evidence we can use, hidden across these battles.

00:03:23.080 --> 00:03:28.480
In 1987's Mighty Thor No. 385, Hulk repeatedly says that Thor can't beat him without his hammer.

00:03:28.480 --> 00:03:32.260
Thor denies it and throws his hammer away twice during the battle,

00:03:32.260 --> 00:03:35.700
But it's only once Mjolnir is back in play that he's able to topple the Hulk.

00:03:35.700 --> 00:03:37.210
That wouldn't mean much on its own,

00:03:37.210 --> 00:03:41.940
But in 2011's Fear Itself No. 5, Thor confesses that he could never beat Hulk

00:03:41.940 --> 00:03:44.040
And it doesn't seem like he's exaggerating either.

00:03:44.060 --> 00:03:47.160
In 2012's Hulk smash Avengers No. 1, all the Avengers

00:03:47.390 --> 00:03:50.780
Collectively work together to try and hold the Hulk down,

00:03:50.780 --> 00:03:53.740
with Thor using the handle of Mjolnir to pin Hulk's neck.

00:03:53.740 --> 00:03:58.900
Hulk sends them all flying, giving us our first true piece of evidence that, in the moment,

00:03:58.920 --> 00:04:02.380
He was stronger than Thor as well as all the other Avengers combined.

00:04:02.380 --> 00:04:06.768
Then again, we are talking about a guy who is canonically defeated by a strategic box of puppies.

00:04:06.768 --> 00:04:11.500
So the title of strongest Avenger might not be as prestigious as it might initially sound.

00:04:11.540 --> 00:04:13.390
Okay, so their head-to-head battles might be a draw,

00:04:13.390 --> 00:04:15.680
But what about comparing their relative feats of strength?

00:04:15.680 --> 00:04:21.000
During Marvel's Secret Wars 4, a cosmic entity known as the Beyonder sends a number of heroes and villains

00:04:21.000 --> 00:04:22.780
to an artificial planet to battle.

00:04:22.780 --> 00:04:26.560
At one point, the Beyonder drops a Mountain range on them

00:04:26.560 --> 00:04:31.260
and our not so Jolly Green Giant literally shoulders the burden while the others plan their escape.

00:04:31.260 --> 00:04:33.880
The mountain range is confirmed to be two miles tall.

00:04:33.880 --> 00:04:41.580
Two miles of pure rock  tall, and the cover itself says that it weighs 150 billion  tons.

00:04:41.580 --> 00:04:47.800
That is three times ten to the 14th power pounds of rock on his shoulders which is incredible,

00:04:47.820 --> 00:04:51.280
But not nearly as incredible as what we see in the Incredible Hulk 102,

00:04:51.280 --> 00:04:55.620
Where he pulls a planet back together, with his bare hands.

00:04:55.630 --> 00:05:00.880
He literally grabs two tectonic plates and yanks them back together.

00:05:00.880 --> 00:05:04.140
My favorite part of this comic by the way, is the explanation for this.

00:05:04.140 --> 00:05:07.720
"He...he shifted the plates." "Of course he did, he's the Hulk."

00:05:07.720 --> 00:05:13.540
Considering the weight of the Earth's crust is about 6,200 times 10 to the 19th pounds,

00:05:13.540 --> 00:05:19.400
and there are about 15 major tectonic plates on earth, each of those plates Hulk is moving is going to add up to

00:05:19.400 --> 00:05:22.820
about 4.1 sextillion pounds,

00:05:22.820 --> 00:05:25.700
and he's moving two of them with his bare hands.

00:05:25.700 --> 00:05:27.800
Good luck standing up to that one Thor.

00:05:27.800 --> 00:05:31.720
Except he does. It seems like he can stand toe-to-toe against this number.

00:05:31.720 --> 00:05:35.220
In Thor 272, Thor and Loki encountered the giant Utgard

00:05:35.220 --> 00:05:40.180
who challenges the Asgardians to some rather unique challenges, one of which is to lift his cat.

00:05:40.200 --> 00:05:42.740
No, no you heard that right. Lift a cat.

00:05:42.760 --> 00:05:45.420
And you all thought that the plot of dark world was bad.

00:05:45.420 --> 00:05:48.560
Look at the source material they're working with here, people. In all seriousness,

00:05:48.560 --> 00:05:52.540
This story is based on Norse mythology and as you may expect the cat isn't

00:05:52.700 --> 00:05:59.660
Actually a cat in both the comic and the myth the cat is actually revealed to be a disguised Midgard serpent

00:06:04.720 --> 00:06:07.500
That probably doesn't mean a whole lot to you.

00:06:07.500 --> 00:06:08.940
Don't worry, it didn't mean a whole lot to me either.

00:06:08.940 --> 00:06:14.340
The serpent is actually a massive snake also known as Jormungandr

00:06:14.340 --> 00:06:16.020
Jormungandr

00:06:16.240 --> 00:06:17.780
JORMUNGANDR

00:06:17.780 --> 00:06:20.480
whose sole purpose is encircling the planet and destroying it.

00:06:20.880 --> 00:06:23.200
Who's a bad kitty snake? No treats for you. Get in the corner.

00:06:23.600 --> 00:06:28.880
More importantly for our purposes, Jörmungandr is massively heavy. Although we don't ever get an exact weight for the serpent,

00:06:29.100 --> 00:06:33.840
we know that it has the mass necessary to surround the entire Earth and destroy it.

00:06:33.840 --> 00:06:35.420
The force that it would take to destroy Earth,

00:06:35.420 --> 00:06:38.960
according to people who have spent a great deal of time thinking about destroying the Earth,

00:06:38.960 --> 00:06:43.280
would be about 2.2 4 times 10 to the 24th pounds of force

00:06:43.340 --> 00:06:47.700
Although Thor fails the test he does manage to lift one of the cat's legs off the ground.

00:06:47.700 --> 00:06:51.000
A feat that is apparently impressive to Utgard the Giant.

00:06:51.000 --> 00:06:53.540
I don't know, I pick up all four of Skip's legs all the time

00:06:53.580 --> 00:06:54.960
And he's getting fatter by the day.

00:06:54.960 --> 00:06:59.860
Anyway that means Thor is exerting the equivalent of five septillion pounds of force,

00:06:59.860 --> 00:07:03.640
a full three more digits than Hulk pulling tectonic plates

00:07:03.840 --> 00:07:09.029
So maybe Thor and Stan Lee are right to think that Thor is in fact the strongest Avenger

00:07:09.029 --> 00:07:10.949
But here's the thing. Comics are weird.

00:07:10.949 --> 00:07:16.880
I mean we just talked about how Thor's strongest feat in the comics is getting a single cat paw off the ground

00:07:16.880 --> 00:07:20.920
Strength scaling in the comics is very different from strength scaling in the movies

00:07:20.920 --> 00:07:22.620
and honestly, this is Film Theory.

00:07:22.620 --> 00:07:24.600
I'm more interested in the movies anyway

00:07:24.600 --> 00:07:27.340
And the MCU operates by a different standard

00:07:27.340 --> 00:07:30.400
A world where Thor isn't a Hitler supporter.

00:07:30.400 --> 00:07:33.860
As Stan Lee said, in the comic universe Thor is literally a god

00:07:33.870 --> 00:07:39.719
But in the MCU Odin makes a point to make it clear that they're not gods. They're mortals just like us

00:07:39.719 --> 00:07:40.900
"We are not gods"

00:07:40.900 --> 00:07:41.699
"We're born"

00:07:41.700 --> 00:07:43.840
"We live. We die, as humans do."

00:07:43.840 --> 00:07:45.760
"Give or take five thousand years"

00:07:45.760 --> 00:07:50.260
And while Asgardians have proven themselves to be quite handy in the MCU like in the original Thor

00:07:50.260 --> 00:07:51.440
"There you go, lass"

00:07:51.440 --> 00:07:54.580
It now puts him in a completely different vein as the Asgardians from earth-616

00:07:54.880 --> 00:07:59.340
That's the main Marvel Comics universe for those of you who are unaware of top-level geek speak.

00:07:59.340 --> 00:08:03.920
So to get our final answer, let's focus on moments of strength from the movies to determine whether

00:08:04.120 --> 00:08:06.580
Ragnarok's computer is just a big troll.

00:08:06.580 --> 00:08:11.100
Our first baseline measurement is Iron Man across the 17 films of the MCU at this point,

00:08:11.180 --> 00:08:14.100
we've seen Tony Stark take on both Hulk and Thor

00:08:14.220 --> 00:08:17.480
So the relative performance against him will be a good benchmark

00:08:17.480 --> 00:08:20.020
The battle against Thor happens in the original Avengers movie.

00:08:20.020 --> 00:08:23.940
During the battle Thor uses his lightning powers to zap the Iron Man suit,

00:08:23.940 --> 00:08:27.400
which has the reverse effect he wanted and charges the armor up to

00:08:27.500 --> 00:08:29.820
475 percent power

00:08:29.820 --> 00:08:31.520
"Power at four hundred percent capacity"

00:08:32.760 --> 00:08:33.680
"How about that?"

00:08:33.680 --> 00:08:35.010
You sure about that Jarvis?

00:08:35.010 --> 00:08:40.710
Then why does it say four seventy-five on Tony's readout? Anyway, Iron Man comes out of this battle alive

00:08:40.810 --> 00:08:42.989
Sure, you could say Thor may have been holding back

00:08:43.000 --> 00:08:47.420
But he was at minimum trying to stop Tony and ultimately failed.

00:08:47.420 --> 00:08:53.020
Compare that now to Hulk vs Iron Man in age of Ultron. Here, Tony uses his special

00:08:53.280 --> 00:08:55.780
Hulk-buster armor, a suit that is powered by

00:08:55.960 --> 00:09:03.719
11 reactor cores. Even with 11 times the output of one normal suit Tony still couldn't overpower Hulk

00:09:03.719 --> 00:09:05.719
"Go to sleep, go to sleep, go to sleep!"

00:09:06.160 --> 00:09:07.980
So we got to give the point to Hulk.

00:09:07.980 --> 00:09:08.480
[Ding]

00:09:08.480 --> 00:09:11.190
And then what about Hulk and Thor's first cinematic battle?

00:09:11.220 --> 00:09:13.740
Here's Thor struggling to hold back Hulk's one arm

00:09:13.740 --> 00:09:18.360
But then here's footage of Thor completely destroying Hulk mere seconds later with Mjolnir,

00:09:18.360 --> 00:09:20.860
which is something we haven't taken into consideration yet.

00:09:20.860 --> 00:09:21.360
The Hammer.

00:09:21.360 --> 00:09:24.220
Why are we spending so much time calculating and comparing,

00:09:24.220 --> 00:09:28.820
when, shouldn't Mjolnir give us our answer outright? Thor can lift Mjolnir when no one else can,

00:09:28.890 --> 00:09:32.240
Thus he should be the strongest right? Well, not exactly.

00:09:32.240 --> 00:09:34.169
You see, if this were the comics that might be the case.

00:09:34.420 --> 00:09:37.760
Comic book Mjolnir has a specific mechanism by which it works,

00:09:37.760 --> 00:09:41.240
As unworthy people attempt to lift it it starts to gain mass

00:09:41.360 --> 00:09:46.120
That leaves open the slim but very real possibility of someone else being able to lift it.

00:09:46.120 --> 00:09:51.840
In fact, Hulk is able to. The hammer gains mass slower than Hulk gains strength as he increasingly

00:09:51.970 --> 00:09:55.500
Gets more angry trying to lift the darn thing. His strength eventually

00:09:55.780 --> 00:09:59.120
Overtakes it and he becomes one of the few to be able to wield Mjolnir

00:09:59.120 --> 00:10:01.619
In the MCU, however, we know mjolnir

00:10:01.620 --> 00:10:05.580
Isn't actually based on weight, but rather on Odin's enchantment?

00:10:05.860 --> 00:10:10.380
"Whosoever holds this hammer, if he be worthy shall possess the power of Thor."

00:10:10.387 --> 00:10:12.279
It's not an issue of mass but rather

00:10:12.649 --> 00:10:16.140
worthiness. the hammer allows itself to be lifted by those deemed worthy.

00:10:16.140 --> 00:10:17.498
When Thor was on the naughty list,

00:10:17.769 --> 00:10:22.568
He couldn't budge the thing. It's only after he's back on the nice list that he's able to lift it again.

00:10:22.779 --> 00:10:25.719
So ability to pick up Mjolnir is a moot point.

00:10:25.720 --> 00:10:28.960
But the hammer itself can help us crown our final winner.

00:10:28.960 --> 00:10:31.660
Back in the Avengers Thor goes toe to toe with the big guy and

00:10:31.660 --> 00:10:36.519
Isn't holding himself back. After Mjolnir knocks Hulk down Hulk attempts to pick it up and retaliate

00:10:36.559 --> 00:10:40.660
But learns as we all know that the owner will only move for those that are deemed worthy.

00:10:40.660 --> 00:10:44.440
The result is Hulk pulling on it with all his might and then this

00:10:45.740 --> 00:10:47.680
 

00:10:47.680 --> 00:10:50.132
I don't remember his feet sinking into any rock.

00:10:50.140 --> 00:10:52.900
After doing some calculations on the size of Hulk's feet,

00:10:52.900 --> 00:10:56.640
and the strength of the tarmac on the S.H.E.I.L.D ship. To dig himself into a floor like this,

00:10:56.649 --> 00:10:59.799
Hulk would have had to output three million seven hundred sixty eight

00:11:00.079 --> 00:11:04.340
Thousand pounds of force to dent the asphalt floors, per individual foot.

00:11:04.340 --> 00:11:07.508
Now compare that to Thor's big moment of strength from the movies,

00:11:07.680 --> 00:11:09.520
Crushing Iron Man's arm.

00:11:09.520 --> 00:11:13.280
At the end of the original Iron Man, Tony corrects a little fallacy about his suit:

00:11:13.280 --> 00:11:16.140
"It's not technically accurate you see it's a gold-titanium alloy..."

00:11:16.320 --> 00:11:19.560
He's not Iron Man, he's gold titanium alloy man.

00:11:19.560 --> 00:11:21.860
In 2016, a team at Rice University

00:11:21.860 --> 00:11:25.980
Discovered a real gold titanium alloy that was four times harder than steel.

00:11:26.060 --> 00:11:29.980
Four times. According to Wikipedia, structural steel can take a force of about

00:11:30.230 --> 00:11:31.639
36 thousand psi

00:11:31.639 --> 00:11:33.740
Before it begins to fall or break.

00:11:33.740 --> 00:11:36.969
So that would mean an alloy four times the strength could take about a hundred and forty four

00:11:36.970 --> 00:11:39.040
thousand pounds of pressure per square inch

00:11:39.040 --> 00:11:44.440
In order for a hand about the size of Thor's to output that same level of pressure to crush Iron Man's gauntlet

00:11:44.440 --> 00:11:47.600
It would translate to three million four hundred fifty six

00:11:47.840 --> 00:11:49.360
thousand pounds of force

00:11:49.440 --> 00:11:54.120
Three hundred thousand pounds of force under what we know holds output of one foot to be

00:11:54.120 --> 00:11:57.240
So any way you slice it, Stan Lee is just outright wrong.

00:11:57.249 --> 00:12:00.700
It's easy to assume that a god-like superhero would be the most overpowered,

00:12:00.700 --> 00:12:03.159
but most of that is coming from Mjolnir itself

00:12:03.160 --> 00:12:06.100
What  Ragnarok plays as a joke at Thor's expense is

00:12:06.110 --> 00:12:07.920
Just factually true

00:12:07.920 --> 00:12:10.300
Based on what's said in the comics, their feats of strength,

00:12:10.699 --> 00:12:13.520
logical comparisons, and just flat-out math and science.

00:12:13.520 --> 00:12:16.860
There is no denying that Hulk is the strongest Avenger

00:12:16.900 --> 00:12:22.000
Well, until we factor vision into all this, then Hulk better start watching out. But Hey,

00:12:22.110 --> 00:12:24.300
That's just a theory!

00:12:24.300 --> 00:12:26.520
A Film Theory.

00:12:26.520 --> 00:12:28.520
Aaaand, Cut

